00:15:11,180 --> 00:15:15,160
In last week's lecture we discussed reasons why people forget things.

153
00:15:15,760 --> 00:15:20,920
This week we will discuss a surprising reason why we might remember some things.

154
00:15:21,660 --> 00:15:25,440
Anxiety. Think about something as simple as buying a coffee.

155
00:15:25,440 --> 00:15:29,840
That may not seem like an experience that would make a deep impression on your

156
00:15:29,840 --> 00:15:32,720
memory, but anxiety could change that.

157
00:15:34,040 --> 00:15:40,140
In fact, a new study suggests that people with higher anxiety levels might remember

158
00:15:40,140 --> 00:15:44,640
certain information better than people with lower anxiety levels.

159
00:15:45,520 --> 00:15:51,120
That's because higher levels of anxiety may make people more susceptible to negative

160
00:15:51,120 --> 00:15:55,120
feelings, putting them in a more negative state of mind.

161
00:15:55,640 --> 00:15:59,860
That, in turn, may make them able to better remember some events.

162
00:16:00,840 --> 00:16:03,740
Let's take a closer look at that new study now.

163
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:10,000
In the study, the researchers started by giving 80 undergraduate students an anxiety

164
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:15,340
test. The test measured the participants' anxiety levels over the preceding two

165
00:16:15,340 --> 00:16:22,280
weeks. Then, to test memory, the participants were shown a series of neutral words,

166
00:16:22,580 --> 00:16:23,500
one at a time.

167
00:16:24,380 --> 00:16:29,820
Some of the words were printed onto photos of negative scenes, meaning images that

168
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:35,280
could affect their emotions negatively, such as a photo of a car accident or a

169
00:16:35,280 --> 00:16:41,270
cemetery. The rest of the were printed onto photos of neutral scenes, such as a

170
00:16:41,270 --> 00:16:43,710
photo of a lake or trees.

171
00:16:44,570 --> 00:16:50,510
Neutral words included words like table or desk that don't elicit emotion.

172
00:16:51,290 --> 00:16:55,190
Later, the participants were asked to think back to the words they were shown

173
00:16:55,190 --> 00:17:00,260
earlier, which caused them to re -enter either a negative or neutral mindset.

174
00:17:01,410 --> 00:17:06,310
The participants were then presented with another set of neutral words, and their

175
00:17:06,310 --> 00:17:08,610
memory of these new words was tested.

176
00:17:08,610 --> 00:17:14,270
The researchers found that the new words presented to people in a negative mindset

177
00:17:14,270 --> 00:17:19,770
were better remembered by people with higher levels of anxiety than those with lower

178
00:17:19,770 --> 00:17:21,090
levels of anxiety.

179
00:17:21,950 --> 00:17:27,010
In other words, when highly anxious individuals took in otherwise emotionally

180
00:17:27,010 --> 00:17:32,050
neutral information that was presented to them, it became colored by their negative

181
00:17:32,050 --> 00:17:35,710
mindset, making them remember the information better.

182
00:17:35,710 --> 00:17:40,610
But these same effects were not seen in people with low levels of

183
00:17:41,770 --> 00:17:46,150
Previous studies have found that extreme levels of anxiety, such as those

184
00:17:46,150 --> 00:17:51,530
experienced by people diagnosed with an anxiety disorder, can be quite detrimental

185
00:17:51,530 --> 00:17:54,230
to memory and cognitive performance.

186
00:17:54,910 --> 00:17:59,910
But the highly anxious people in this study represent individuals who are managing

187
00:17:59,910 --> 00:18:04,250
their anxiety, and for whom anxiety is not a serious problem.

