00:23:19,690 --> 00:23:24,270
Which is interesting when considering the extent to which people sacrifice

240
00:23:24,270 --> 00:23:28,930
friendships, or at least the time they spend with friends, because of the extended

241
00:23:28,930 --> 00:23:30,850
hours they're devoting to work.

242
00:23:31,310 --> 00:23:36,270
Just last week, I was remarking to a colleague that I'm content with only one social

243
00:23:36,270 --> 00:23:37,590
engagement per week.

244
00:23:38,130 --> 00:23:42,570
But according to recent research, that's evidently not enough.

245
00:23:42,570 --> 00:23:48,810
In an initial study of more than 700 respondents, scholars from an American

246
00:23:48,810 --> 00:23:54,710
university analyzed the impact that friends, as opposed to family, have on self

247
00:23:54,710 --> 00:23:56,510
-esteem and well -being.

248
00:23:56,890 --> 00:23:59,430
Friends came out substantially on top.

249
00:24:00,050 --> 00:24:05,530
That's because to be someone's mate is a voluntary act, unlike family, who people

250
00:24:05,530 --> 00:24:06,790
rarely get to choose.

251
00:24:06,790 --> 00:24:12,170
The researchers found that when people choose to cultivate and maintain supportive

252
00:24:12,170 --> 00:24:13,950
friendships with an individual,

253
00:24:14,610 --> 00:24:18,690
it means that the person is valued and worthy of their limited time.

254
00:24:19,050 --> 00:24:23,170
Such sentiments of value and worthiness boost our self -esteem.

255
00:24:24,370 --> 00:24:28,190
The second study comprised more than 300 participants.

256
00:24:28,810 --> 00:24:33,450
It proved that the better we feel about ourselves, the more likely we'll perform our

257
00:24:33,450 --> 00:24:35,690
job confidently and competently.

258
00:24:35,690 --> 00:24:41,730
This follow -up study found that non -work friends even improve people's job

259
00:24:41,730 --> 00:24:47,570
satisfaction. They have as much of an impact on how much they love their jobs as do

260
00:24:47,570 --> 00:24:52,610
the friends they have at work, despite not actually being at our place of work.

261
00:24:53,330 --> 00:24:58,570
These types of friends tend to be our preferred outlet for venting about work

262
00:24:58,570 --> 00:25:03,850
-related matters. This is an avenue that may not be available at the office.

263
00:25:03,850 --> 00:25:09,770
So even though friendships can be easy to neglect when confronted by pressures at

264
00:25:09,770 --> 00:25:12,430
work, or even pressures at home,

265
00:25:13,110 --> 00:25:17,070
neglecting our friends can turn out to be harmful and counterproductive.

266
00:25:17,890 --> 00:25:23,270
That's why, when determining how to create a better work -life balance, we need to

267
00:25:23,270 --> 00:25:29,490
consider not only how to balance work and family demands, but also how to cultivate

268
00:25:29,490 --> 00:25:32,050
and sustain supportive friendships.

269
00:25:32,050 --> 00:25:36,930
It's for this reason that managers should never discriminate when it comes to

270
00:25:36,930 --> 00:25:40,590
requests from employees for flexible work arrangements.

271
00:25:41,350 --> 00:25:46,930
It's irrelevant whether their need for a desired schedule is due to, say, parenting

272
00:25:46,930 --> 00:25:50,910
responsibilities or a craving to hang out with a best mate.

273
00:25:51,510 --> 00:25:57,090
What matters is the opportunity to engage in a nourishing activity outside of work

274
00:25:57,090 --> 00:26:00,950
that will definitely have a follow -on effect at work.

